/**
 * The places where a transliteration as served does not spell the Arabic,
 * put right on the way to the screen.
 *
 * ── WHY THERE IS A LIST ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The three risan/quran-json editions are generated by rule from the
 * Arabic and say of themselves that no reader has reviewed them. So they
 * were checked here, every ayah: the Latin text derived a second time from
 * the bundled Arabic (Tanzil's Uthmani text) by an independent program,
 * and compared letter for letter — consonants, vowels, lengths, doubling,
 * the joins between words (tools/transliteration-check, 2026-11-24).
 * 6,222 of the 6,236 ayahs are what the Arabic says. The fourteen below
 * are not, and in each the Arabic settles it:
 *
 *   word — a letter or a syllable of the word is missing or wrong
 *          (yuḥyiya written yuḥyi; nunjī written nujī; dhambin, a tanwīn,
 *          written dhambim before a letter that takes no m);
 *   join — the word is right and its join to the next is not the one the
 *          edition writes everywhere else (ḥayātin wa- for ḥayātiw wa-).
 *
 * The same fourteen are wrong in all three editions, which are one text in
 * three spellings (checked: the Indonesian and the plain English are the
 * scholarly one respelt, in every ayah).
 *
 * ── HOW IT IS APPLIED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * A correction replaces one fragment of one ayah, and only when that
 * fragment is there: should the source put an ayah right itself, the
 * fragment is gone and the correction does nothing. It is applied to what
 * is read, wherever it was read from — the mirror, the source, or the
 * device — so the stored text stays byte for byte what the source served.
 *
 * The licence (CC BY-SA 4.0) asks that changes be said: they are, here,
 * in docs/data-sources.md and under Settings → About.
 *
 * ── ABAY'S TURKISH ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Muhammet Abay's edition is a person's work, not a program's, and the
 * same check was run on it: 5,900 ayahs are the Arabic letter for letter;
 * 185 differ only in where he pauses; 23 write a length the script shows
 * and the reading does not (enâ, ûlî, lâkinnâ) — his convention, left as
 * it is; and 113 have a wrong letter, a wrong length or a wrong word: a
 * plain letter for an emphatic or the reverse (muḥsenât for muḥṣanât,
 * liyaḳdiye for liyaḳḍiye), an article not assimilated (lildîni, felnâru),
 * a singular for Ḥafṣ's plural (seyyietü, bişehâdetihim), a letter dropped
 * (keŝîâ, rasûâ), and in 29:13 and 29:46 words that are not the ayah's.
 *
 * Those 113 are put right here too (the maintainer's decision, 2026-11-25),
 * the same way: a fragment of an ayah, only when it is there, in his own
 * spelling — the corrected word written as he writes it elsewhere. Each was
 * proved by the check that found it: with the fragment replaced the ayah
 * reads as the Arabic does (tools/transliteration-check/fixes_tr.py).
 * Nothing else of his text is changed, the stored and mirrored files stay
 * as served, and Settings → About says how many ayahs are corrected.
 */

/** [surah, ayah, as served, as the Arabic reads] */
type Correction = readonly [number, number, string, string];

